course: dictionary
lesson: 16162
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. You can tell which meaning is right by reading the words around the word. These words are its context, and they leave clues.
explain: «In woodwork class, we measured and sawed a piece of pine.» Here woodwork means the school subject of making things out of wood. The clues are class, measured and sawed.
explain: «The woodwork in the old house was painted white.» Here woodwork means the parts of a building made of wood, such as doors, window frames and stairs. The clue is the house, which tells you what kind of wood is meant.
fact: Woodwork is an uncountable noun, so we say «some woodwork», not «a woodwork».
fact: «Woodwork» can mean a school subject, the wooden parts of a building, or the goal posts and crossbar in football.
fact: In UK football, «hit the woodwork» means the ball struck the goal post or crossbar.
fact: «Come out of the woodwork» means to appear suddenly, often when people do not want them to.
fact: A good check is to swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
exercise: «In woodwork, Ella learned to saw a straight line.» What is woodwork here? | A tool for cutting paper | A kind of wooden bird | Painting a wall in a hurry | *The school subject of making things out of wood
exercise: «The ball hit the woodwork and bounced away.» What was hit? | The grass on the pitch | *The goal post or crossbar | The window of the pavilion | The net behind the goal
exercise: «Dad spent the weekend painting the woodwork in the hall.» What does woodwork mean here? | The ceiling lights | *The doors, window frames and stairs made of wood | The brick walls of the house | The floor tiles
test: «Suddenly, old friends came out of the woodwork when the news spread.» What does come out of the woodwork mean? | To win a prize | To hide away quietly | To make furniture for a school | *To appear suddenly, often when they are not wanted
test: Woodwork is an uncountable noun. Which sentence is correct? | I learned the woodworks at school | I learned a woodwork at school | I learned three woodwork at school | *I learned some woodwork at school
exam: «The woodwork of the old church was carved by hand.» What does woodwork mean here? | Hitting a ball off a post | *The wooden parts of the building, such as doors and stairs | Sudden, unwanted visitors | A school lesson about wood
